+
+# Decision: Whole-visit revisions for concurrent visit edits
+
+## Context
+
+Record-store defines how a visit changes when independently edited copies of the same visit arrive from different field workers. Visits are identified by a client-generated UUID; tablets can be offline for 48 hours; two coordinators resolve questions about submitted observations. When two edits to the same visit derive from the same starting state, the store must decide what the visit's current state becomes — and it must do so without discarding any submitted observation and without treating arrival order as authority.
+
+What is at stake if this decision is wrong: an automatic merge that combines two field edits into a record nobody submitted fails the team's tenet that no submitted edit is silently discarded in spirit — the combined record silently discards both workers' intent, even though both payloads are retained. Conversely, a revision policy that drowns the two coordinators in review work for trivially unrelated edits threatens the tenet that the team's workflow operates without a dedicated systems administrator. Downstream, tablet-sync and review-export size their storage, retry, review, and export-eligibility designs around the outcome, so reversal after they are built is expensive.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates**
+
+- **No discarded submissions** (inherited, from HLD tenet "Do not silently discard a submitted edit"): whatever the policy does with concurrent edits, every original submission must remain retrievable. Eliminates any overwrite-last-wins policy.
+- **No invented scientific combinations** (doc-local): the server may not produce field values that no worker submitted. Automatic merging produces such values whenever it combines fields whose scientific relationship the rules do not model (a species identification revision and its cover estimate, for example). Eliminates any policy that combines overlapping-field content without coordinator approval.
+
+**Weighted criteria**
+
+- **Whole-visit intent preserved** (major): a submitted visit is a unit — the worker observed date, observers, cover, and notes together. A policy that preserves the submitted unit keeps the path from field observation to exported value inspectable (HLD tenet). A policy that decomposes visits into field changes loses the unit even when every field is retained.
+- **Merge-rule maintenance burden** (major): rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries must be written, kept current, and — critically — kept *scientifically literate*, since a technically correct merge of two fields can still be scientifically wrong. This burden falls on a team with no systems administrator (HLD tenet).
+- **Review volume** (moderate): how much coordinator work competing edits generate. Unrelated field edits create review work under whole-visit revisions; under automatic merge they resolve themselves.
+- **Submission size and tablet complexity** (minor): whole-visit revisions re-send complete payloads and retain a base revision ID; structured-change merging sends deltas and retains base snapshots. Tablet storage is small relative to either.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Every submission carries a complete visit payload and names the base revision it was made against. The server appends each accepted submission to an immutable, server-ordered revision chain per visit. A submission against the current head becomes the new head. A submission against any other base becomes a *competing revision*: stored in full, never applied to current state, and marking the visit *unresolved* until a coordinator resolution selects one competing revision as the head. Losing revisions remain as history. The server never merges, reorders, or partially applies payloads.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A corrects an observer name against revision 4; worker B, offline at the time, adds a missing species also against revision 4. Both arrive as complete revision-4-based payloads. The visit is unresolved with two competing revisions; a coordinator compares them side by side and selects one — or the team accepts that the other worker's edit must be re-submitted against the new head if it is still needed.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- No discarded submissions: **passes** — every submission is stored immutable.
+- No invented scientific combinations: **passes** — current state is always some worker's complete submission.
+- Whole-visit intent: **strong** — the review screen shows complete visits exactly as submitted.
+- Merge-rule maintenance: **strong** — there are no merge rules.
+- Review volume: **weak** — every concurrent edit pair, including unrelated-field edits, creates review work.
+- Submission size / tablet complexity: **partial** — complete payloads re-sent; tablets retain only the base revision ID.
+
+**Summary.** Trades coordinator review volume for zero merge logic and intact whole-visit semantics.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission carries its base visit state and structured changes (field deltas). The server computes each edit's changes against the shared base and applies a maintained rule set: changes to disjoint fields combine automatically into a new record; changes to the same field conflict and require coordinator review. Original payloads are retained; combined records carry field-level provenance naming which edit contributed each field.
+
+**Demonstration.** Worker A corrects an observer name; worker B adds a missing species, both against revision 4. The rules see disjoint field sets and combine them into revision 5 with provenance (`observer`: A's edit; `species`: B's edit). No coordinator involvement. Had both edited the same species entry, the visit would be unresolved for that field.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- No discarded submissions: **passes** — payloads retained with provenance.
+- No invented scientific combinations: **eliminated** — automatic combination of disjoint fields can produce records nobody submitted when the fields are scientifically linked (a revised identification and its cover estimate look like disjoint fields to any rule keyed on field names).
+- Whole-visit intent: **weak** — the unit of submission is decomposed into field changes; a combined record is a synthesis, not an observation.
+- Merge-rule maintenance: **weak** — rules for scalars, observer lists, and species entries must be maintained, and rule correctness is scientific correctness, which the server cannot judge.
+- Review volume: **strong** — unrelated edits never reach the coordinators.
+- Submission size / tablet complexity: **strong** — deltas are small, but tablets retain base snapshots and implement structured-change generation.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a maintained, scientifically fragile rule set and decomposition of submitted intent for a much smaller review queue.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.** The field-aware option fails the no-invented-scientific-combinations gate: automatically combining disjoint-looking fields can silently synthesize records that no worker observed, and the team judged that failure mode — a merge rule combining scientifically related edits without noticing — worse than a larger review queue, which the two coordinators can operate. The review-volume cost is real and accepted deliberately: unrelated edits to the same visit will create review work. The decision turns on the tenets *Do not silently discard a submitted edit* (in its spirit, not just its letter) and *Prefer workflows two coordinators can operate* — a maintained merge rule set fails the second tenet because its upkeep requires expertise the team does not staff.
+
+Committing forecloses automatic field-level combination in record-store for the first release; if review volume proves unsustainable, the alternative can be revisited against the same criteria — the gates above would still eliminate any merge that combines overlapping scientific content without approval, so a revisit would target only disjoint-field merges where scientific linkage is genuinely absent, and would have to re-litigate whole-visit intent.
+
+Committing obligates downstream segments (cascade, not co-ownership — both must link back to record-store rather than invent concurrency semantics):
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must retain the base revision ID of each pending edit, re-send complete payloads on retry (idempotency keys make retries safe), and explain unresolved visits and pending competing revisions to field workers.
+- **Review-export** must build a revision-comparison review view, record resolutions as coordinator-identity + contributing-submission outcomes, and hold unresolved visits out of the export set.
+
+One question is deliberately deferred to review-export, which owns the coordinator interface it would change: whether a coordinator may resolve by recording a manually composed payload instead of selecting one competing revision. Until then, resolution selects one revision.
+
+## References
+
+- docs/intent/record-store/record-store-design.md — the design this decision resolves
+- docs/high-level-design.md — tenets this decision turns on
